# Reviewed history blobs that predate the private-marker rule.
#
# What this is
#   tools/public-scan --git-history --private-markers rejects any blob holding
#   a repository-private word. The rule was added after those words had already
#   been published, so it also condemns commits that shipped before it existed.
#   The published history cannot be rewritten: the repository is public, clones
#   and forks already carry these objects, and rewriting would break every
#   reference to them while removing nothing from anyone's copy. Each blob below
#   was read, classified, and accepted as history that stays.
#
# What it is not
#   A blob listed here is exempt from the private-marker rule and from nothing
#   else. Credential and key patterns still fail for every blob, listed or not.
#   The exemption is keyed to the exact object id, and an object id is a digest
#   of the content, so it cannot be stretched to cover anything but the reviewed
#   bytes. Editing a listed file produces a different object that fails again.
#
# What was reviewed (2026-08-11)
#   33 blobs  a maintainer's personal name, written before shipped files were
#             required to name roles rather than people. The working tree was
#             cleaned; these are the pre-cleanup copies.
#    4 blobs  a private word appearing only inside a longer underscore-joined
#             identifier, which the rule tests part by part.
#   No blob in this list holds a credential, key, token, or host secret.
#
# The rule for changing this file
#   Additive only, and only after review. Append an entry when a blob already
#   published is confirmed to hold nothing worse than the classes above, and say
#   so in the commit. Never add a blob to make a new failure go away: a failure
#   on an unpublished blob means the working tree still has to be fixed. Removing
#   an entry is safe at any time and only ever makes the scan stricter.
#
# Format: <40-character blob object id>  <path the blob was found at>

